# 12. 🔄 Docker Image Layers

Docker images are built using multiple layers.

For example:

```dockerfile
FROM python:3.12-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install -r requirements.txt

COPY . .

CMD ["python", "app.py"]
```

Conceptually:

```text
┌─────────────────────────┐
│ CMD                     │
├─────────────────────────┤
│ COPY application        │
├─────────────────────────┤
│ RUN pip install         │
├─────────────────────────┤
│ COPY requirements.txt   │
├─────────────────────────┤
│ WORKDIR                 │
├─────────────────────────┤
│ Python Base Image       │
└─────────────────────────┘
```

Docker can reuse unchanged layers during subsequent builds.

This is called **build cache**.

---

# 13. ⚡ Docker Build Cache

Suppose we have:

```dockerfile
COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install -r requirements.txt

COPY . .
```

If only application code changes:

```text
requirements.txt
       ↓
unchanged
       ↓
pip install layer
       ↓
CACHE USED
       ↓
application layer rebuilt
```

This makes builds faster.

### Best Practice

Place instructions that change less frequently before instructions that change frequently.

